# Activation object size: what is feasible, and what it would cost
# Host DESKTOP-CBL5NUA (Desktop 2, Ryzen 7 8700G), 2026-09-20, env mlir-aie-iron
# OFFLINE. No container was built at any object size but 6,400 B, no hardware context was
# opened, and xrt-smi was not consulted because nothing here touches the device.
#
# Command:
#   python tools/activation_object_sweep.py --sizes 4032,7920,7200,8000,8800,9600
#
# WHAT IS MEASURED AND WHAT IS DERIVED
#   MEASURED  the 6,400 B control row: 2,972 (yolov8n) and 7,143 (yolov8s) DMA tasks per
#             frame, asserted by the tool against the counts read off silicon in
#             results/aie/split_segment_floor_phoenix_20260920.log. The floor split
#             (transfer / per-task) is from results/aie/object_size_repricing_20260920.log.
#   DERIVED   every row above 6,400 B. The schedule is real - the compiler is pointed at the
#             candidate geometry and asked to emit - but the pricing applies measured
#             constants to a counted workload. It is not a measurement of that container.
#
# WHY THIS RAN
#   results/aie/notes_yolov8s_gap.md records "the best object size found over 541 candidates
#   is 7,920 B, worth -0.265 ms on yolov8s and +0.143 ms on yolov8n". The script behind that
#   sweep was never committed (git log -S finds nothing); only the quoted cost model survives.
#   results/aie/object_size_repricing_20260920.log showed that model over-counted transport
#   3.10x by charging MemTile-to-core bytes at the DDR rate, which moved its optimum upward.
#   This run replaces the lost sweep with the real scheduler.

## 1. 7,920 B cannot be built

An a_pattern is ONE strided BD whose extent must equal the object size exactly, and a packet
carries ncin planes of rows_in x cols_in x 8 bytes. rows_in and cols_in MAY exceed what a kind
needs - the surplus is junk inside the plane - so the size is not confined to multiples of the
plane sizes in use today. Two conditions bind instead.

  k1 reads the output tile's own pixels: 5 x 20 x 8 = 800 B per plane, no halo, nothing to
  trim and nothing to pad without reading a neighbouring tile's columns. So the object wants
  to be a multiple of 800.

  k3s1 is pinned at ncin = 4 by weight capacity (k*k*ncin*256 <= 9,216 gives k1 <= 36,
  k3 <= 4, k5 <= 1). Four planes of whole pixels means A_BYTES / 8 must divide by 4, so the
  object must be a multiple of 32.

7,920 = 2^4 x 495 is a multiple of 16 and not of 32. 7,920 / 8 = 990 pixels, and 990 / 4 is
247.5, so k3s1 would drop to ncin = 3 and emit a third more chunks - about +764 tasks on
yolov8s by itself. 7,920 was a scalar with no descriptor behind it.

The ceiling above is core data memory. results/model_zoo/memory_yolov8s.json reads 59,392 B
used of 65,536 on every compute tile, with TWO 6,400 B activation buffers (depth 2), so the
6,144 B of headroom allows at most 6,400 + 3,072 = 9,472 B. 9,600 is listed below anyway; it
needs 256 B freed elsewhere on the tile before it could be built at all.

That leaves 7,200, 8,000 and 8,800 as buildable today, and 9,600 behind a tile-memory change.

## 2. Packets are not tasks, and that is what the lost sweep got wrong second

The sweep priced "roughly 5.1 ms of issue for 8,772 merged quad tasks", i.e. tasks in
proportion to packets. They are not proportional. merge_quad folds a quad's four fills into
one task and the scheduler keeps folding along whatever BD dimensions remain under the 4-D
ceiling, so a kind whose pattern is 2-D folds far deeper than one that is 3-D:

| kind | pattern today | packets per DMA task, yolov8n | yolov8s |
|---|---|---:|---:|
| k3s2 | (16, 400), two-dimensional | 23.60 | 39.63 |
| k1 | (8, 5, 160) | 4.90 | 4.71 |
| k3s1 | (4, 8, 200) | 4.42 | 4.09 |
| pool | (2, 16, 200) | 4.00 | 4.00 |
| res | (8, 5, 160) | 4.00 | 4.00 |

The per-kind split is an attribution, not an assertion: items the scheduler rebuilds during a
merge lose their kind and are pooled, which overcounts the activation total by about 8%
against the asserted figure. Only the control totals are checked against silicon.

The consequence is direct. Raising k3s2 to two input blocks per packet - the obvious use of a
bigger object, and the one that looked free - costs it its second dimension. Built that way at
8,800 B, k3s2 went from 283 to 1,290 tasks on yolov8s, swamping k1's 303-task saving and
pushing the whole frame from 7,143 to 8,335 tasks. Every candidate below therefore keeps k3s2
and k5s1 two-dimensional, at one input block per packet with junk rows.

So 6,400 B is not an arbitrary constant that nobody justified. It is the largest object that
keeps the kind with the biggest plane at a single plane, and therefore two-dimensional, and
therefore merging about 40 packets into one task.

