## 4. Reading it

Net is the derived change in the dispatch floor: the task term at the model's measured
microseconds per task, plus the transport term at the measured 6.898931 GB/s per column over
four columns. Core bytes are converted to wire bytes by the model's own measured core:shim
ratio (3.614 on yolov8n, 4.685 on yolov8s), which is the weakest step here - it assumes the
MemTile re-serves the extra bytes in the same proportion it re-serves today's.

"Best case" subtracts the up2 bound. Every candidate generates the k1up2 source block as rows
of 25 pixels rather than today's 20, because no multiple of 800 is also a multiple of the
640 B four-row source block. The core's up2 expansion would have to be taught the wider row.
Running the control with the generated shape instead of the real one costs 48 (yolov8n) and
224 (yolov8s) tasks, so that is the most a candidate row can be overstating.

EVERY BUILDABLE CANDIDATE IS A REGRESSION. The single corner that goes negative is yolov8s at
8,800 B in the best case, -0.153 ms, which requires the up2 uncertainty to resolve entirely in
its favour, still sits under the 0.29 ms bar the yolov8s gap was accepted at, and comes with
+0.064 ms on yolov8n in the same best case. 9,600 B is worse than 8,800 on both models and
additionally needs 256 B of tile memory freed.

The shape of the answer is that the task saving is small and bounded while the byte penalty is
large and linear. Only k1 and k1up2 can raise ncin at all; k3s1 is already at its weight-
capacity maximum of 4, k5s1 at 1, and pool and res are fixed by their semantics. So a bigger
object buys fewer tasks on about a third of the traffic and pays 12 to 41 per cent more bytes
on all of it.

## 5. What would change this

  - Going SMALLER was already priced a loss in notes_yolov8s_gap.md, and the re-pricing made
    the task term 2.1 to 2.6x bigger, so smaller is now a worse loss, not a better one. The
    floor is k3s2's own minimum plane of 11 rows x 41 pixels x 8 = 3,608 B.
  - The weight capacity cap is the real lever, not the object size. If k3s1 could carry more
    than 4 input blocks, a bigger object would pay. That is a weight-packet question
    (W_MAX_BYTES = 9,216), not an activation-packet one.
  - A per-kind object size would dissolve the whole constraint, and is the thing
    docs/DECISIONS.md already records as needing MemTile channels the column does not have.
    That recorded reason is correct but it is not the binding one; the binding one is above.

## 6. Not done here

  No container was built. No dispatch was run. The 6,400 B control is the only row with
  silicon behind it, and it is behind it only as a task count.
